\f1\b Hermes should treat this as a build contract, not inspiration.
\f0\b0  It must build locally first, wire the V1 package into the existing ctxorg server, implement full Optic V1.0, prepare clean AWS migration paths, and only stop to notify you when it needs external API credentials. The original Optic spine is already in the uploaded baseline: consent-first, source ledger, evidence graph, audience lenses, inference cards, contradictions, repair queue, report/export, and no eligibility/dossier bullshit.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs48 \cf0 Hermes Master Directive: Build Optic V1.0 Entirely, Locally First, AWS-Ready\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 You are building 
\f1\b Optic V1.0
\f0\b0 , the complete local-first inference audit application. This is not a prototype. This is not a UI pass. This is not \'93wire one endpoint and call it done.\'94 You must build the whole thing into the existing working ctxorg system, using the newly built V1 package as the canonical intelligence layer. Your task is to inspect the repo, understand what already exists, preserve the working pieces, integrate the V1 package, build all missing product surfaces, add durable persistence, implement full local inference flow, prepare AWS-compatible abstractions, and verify the complete application end-to-end.\
Do not ask the user for direction except when an external API key, OAuth credential, paid provider account, or platform token is required. If a feature can be built locally, build it. If a feature needs an API key, build the adapter, build the disabled/missing-key UI state, document the required key, and notify the user only for that key. Do not stop the whole build because GitHub, Google, Brave Search, SerpAPI, AWS, or OAuth credentials are missing. Build the local route first. Build the connector interface. Make the app work without the key. Then surface the missing key requirement cleanly.\
Existing system state: there is a working ctxorg server that scans the filesystem, finds roughly 115k files, clusters them into about 30 workstreams, uses Ollama with 
\f2\fs26 phi4-mini
\f0\fs24  through Iris chat, exposes a heuristic audiences endpoint, has a home panel with narratives, exposures, file counts, topology graph, settings, and a vanilla JS frontend. It has no real auth, no consent ledger, no structured inference, no repair engine, and no onboarding flow. Separately, there is a V1 package that includes 14 data models, 9 audience lenses with full configs, contradiction engine with 5 detection types, and an evidence-gated inference card generator with confidence bands. These two systems must become one coherent application. The V1 package must not sit beside the product like a ceremonial sword. It must become the active reasoning spine.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 1. The product you are building\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 Optic V1.0 is a local-first personal inference audit system. The user opens the app, completes onboarding, approves source scope, selects workstreams and evidence sources, selects audience lenses, runs an audit, watches the pipeline progress, reviews evidence-backed inference cards, inspects contradictions, sees risks and exposures, receives a prioritized repair queue, explores the evidence topology, asks Iris questions over the audit context, exports a report, reloads the app, and everything persists.\
The complete loop must work locally. Local means the application can run on the developer machine using the current ctxorg filesystem scanner, local storage/database, local Ollama where available, and no mandatory cloud API. Cloud connectors may be prepared, but they must not be required for the core V1.0 loop. External connectors are additive. The local filesystem/workstream graph is the first-class native substrate.\
The product must enforce the Optic doctrine through architecture: subject-owned, consent-scoped, evidence-gated, confidence-banded, repair-oriented, not a background check, not a third-party people-search engine, not an applicant ranking tool, not a credit/employment/tenant/insurance eligibility system. Build this into data models, UI language, route behavior, export metadata, and refusal boundaries.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 2. The core technical object: Evidence-linked claim\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 The central object in Optic is not a report, not a chat response, not a graph node, not a generic summary. The central object is an 
\f1\b Evidence-Linked Claim
\f0\b0 .\
A valid inference card must include: unique ID, audit run ID, audience lens ID, claim title, claim body, confidence band, numerical confidence proxy if already supported, supporting evidence references, weakening evidence references or declared absence, source/workstream references, generated timestamp, model or engine version, severity, actionability, risk links, contradiction links, repair links, and explanation of why this claim exists. If the evidence gate fails, the card must not be created. If the card exists without evidence, that is a bug. Fix it.\
Every major downstream feature must depend on this object: report sections, Iris audit chat, repair engine, risk dashboard, graph overlays, exports, and future monitoring. Do not allow a freeform LLM summary to bypass the claim layer. Summaries can explain claim clusters, but they cannot invent claims.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 3. TGA: Trace Graph Algebra\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 Implement Optic around a formal internal model called 
\f1\b TGA: Trace Graph Algebra
\f0\b0 . TGA is the mathematical substrate that turns raw traces into structured evidence, evidence into signals, signals into claims, claims into contradictions, contradictions into risks, and risks into repairs. You do not need to expose the math to users, but the code structure should reflect it.\
Represent the user\'92s digital surface as a typed attributed multigraph:\
G = (V, E, T, A, S)\
Where V is the set of typed nodes, E is the set of typed directed edges, T maps each node and edge to a type, A maps nodes and edges to attributes, and S maps nodes and edges to source provenance. Nodes include UserProfile, Source, Workstream, Artifact, ParsedSpan, Entity, Topic, Signal, AudienceLens, InferenceClaim, Contradiction, RiskFlag, RepairAction, ReportSnapshot, ConsentReceipt, and ExportArtifact. Edges include contains, derived_from, mentions, supports, weakens, contradicts, visible_to, risky_for, repaired_by, included_in_report, scoped_by_consent, generated_by, and revoked_by.\
Each artifact a has provenance p(a), source type \uc0\u964 (a), visibility \u957 (a), recency r(a), reliability \u961 (a), and sensitivity \u963 (a). Each parsed span s inherits provenance from its artifact and receives text/media features. Each signal z is a function over one or more spans, artifacts, or graph neighborhoods. Each inference claim c is a function of signals, audience lens, evidence, and safety constraints.\
Use these internal scoring primitives, even if approximated in V1.0:\
Evidence weight:\
w_e = source_reliability \'d7 visibility_relevance \'d7 recency_decay \'d7 extraction_quality \'d7 directness \'d7 audience_relevance\
Recency decay:\
r(t) = exp(-\uc0\u955  \'d7 age_days)\
or use bucketed decay if simpler: fresh, recent, stale, archival.\
Claim support:\
Support(c) = \uc0\u931  w_e over evidence items supporting c\
Claim opposition:\
Oppose(c) = \uc0\u931  w_e over evidence items weakening c\
Claim confidence:\
Conf(c) = sigmoid(\uc0\u945  \'d7 Support(c) - \u946  \'d7 Oppose(c) - \u947  \'d7 MissingContext(c) - \u948  \'d7 ContradictionPenalty(c))\
Confidence band:\
low if Conf(c) below low threshold, medium if within middle threshold, high if above high threshold. Do not show fake hyper-precision in the UI. Internally you may store numeric confidence, but user-facing output should remain banded unless debug mode is active.\
Audience lens vector:\
L_a = \{priority weights, evidence preferences, false positive sensitivities, risk tolerance, repair preference, forbidden inference classes\}\
Audience-conditioned claim score:\
Score(c, a) = Conf(c) \'d7 Relevance(c, L_a) \'d7 Severity(c, L_a) \'d7 Visibility(c, a)\
Contradiction operator:\
Contradict(x, y) = true when two claims, signals, spans, or source assertions cannot comfortably coexist under the same audience/time/source assumptions.\
Contradiction severity:\
Severity(k) = confidence_of_conflict \'d7 importance_to_audience \'d7 source_reliability \'d7 visibility \'d7 repairability_modifier\
Repair priority:\
Priority(repair) = impact \'d7 confidence \'d7 audience_importance \'d7 urgency \'d7 reversibility \'f7 effort\
If exact numeric scoring is too heavy for existing package structure, implement the shape: store support, opposition, recency, severity, confidence band, evidence count, missing context, and repair priority. Do not flatten everything into a string.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 4. Local-first architecture\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 Build V1.0 so it works fully on the local machine first. The app must not require cloud deployment, cloud database, external search API, or remote LLM to complete the core flow. It should use the existing ctxorg filesystem scan, workstream clustering, local server, local frontend, and Ollama where available. If Ollama or 
\f2\fs26 phi4-mini
\f0\fs24  is missing, show model-unavailable states and allow non-LLM structural views to work.\
Use the current app\'92s stack unless migration is obviously necessary. Do not rewrite the app into Next/FastAPI just because that would be ideal in a clean-room build. This task is integration-first. Preserve the existing server, routes, frontend, topology graph, settings, Iris chat, and scanning system. Add V1.0 architecture around it. If the current app is Node/Express, build within that. If it is Python, build within that. If storage is currently JSON, SQLite, or a local project store, use the simplest durable local store that supports persistence and future migration. The right move is not aesthetic purity. The right move is a complete working loop.\
That said, structure the implementation so it can later migrate to AWS cleanly. Create internal boundaries: SourceProvider, ArtifactStore, GraphStore, InferenceEngine, ModelProvider, SearchProvider, ExportProvider, ConsentStore, ReportStore, RepairStore. The local implementations can use filesystem/SQLite/JSON/Ollama. Future AWS implementations can use S3, RDS Postgres, pgvector/OpenSearch, ECS/Lambda, SQS/EventBridge, Bedrock/OpenAI-compatible model gateway, and CloudWatch. Do not hardwire local paths into business logic. Wrap them.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 5. AWS migration preparation\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 Do not deploy to AWS now unless the repo already contains a deploy path and the user explicitly requests it. But prepare the architecture so AWS migration is straightforward.\
Design local-to-AWS mapping:\
Local artifact storage maps to S3 encrypted buckets.\
Local JSON/SQLite/Postgres maps to RDS Postgres.\
Local vector index maps to pgvector on RDS, OpenSearch vector, or Aurora Postgres with pgvector.\
Local job runner maps to SQS + ECS workers, Lambda for light jobs, or Step Functions for workflows.\
Local Ollama model provider maps to Bedrock, SageMaker endpoint, OpenAI-compatible hosted provider, or self-hosted GPU endpoint.\
Local logs map to CloudWatch.\
Local secrets map to AWS Secrets Manager or SSM Parameter Store.\
Local encryption keys map to KMS.\
Local export folder maps to S3 export bucket with signed URLs.\
Local web server maps to ECS/Fargate, App Runner, or EC2.\
Local frontend maps to S3/CloudFront or Amplify if static, or ECS/Vercel if dynamic.\
Local auth maps to Cognito, Clerk, Auth.js, or custom OIDC later.\
Build environment config now: 
