Prepare infra docs or config stubs if appropriate, but do not spend the whole build writing Terraform while the app itself is broken. Product loop first, migration path second.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 6. Persistence requirements\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 The app must persist everything needed to reload the product state. At minimum persist: local user/profile, onboarding state, consent receipts, source registry, workstream registry, artifact references, parsed evidence summaries, selected audience lenses, audit config, audit runs, audit status, inference cards, contradictions, risks/exposures, repair actions, reports, exports, model settings, and UI settings.\
If the current ctxorg app already has persistent storage, extend it. If not, add a simple local database. SQLite is acceptable for local V1.0. Version the schema. Create migrations or a schema initialization routine. Do not rely on in-memory arrays. Do not store the entire product state in frontend localStorage except lightweight UI preferences. The backend must be the source of truth.\
Every audit run should be a snapshot. A report generated today should not silently mutate tomorrow because the filesystem changed. If the user rescans, create a new audit run or update source freshness explicitly. Reports must be reopenable from history.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 7. Onboarding flow\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 Build onboarding as a real state machine, not a decorative slideshow.\
Required onboarding steps:\
Step 1: Welcome and product boundary. Explain Optic is local-first self-audit, not a third-party evaluator.\
Step 2: Local profile setup. Create local user/profile object. In single-user local mode, this can be simple: display name, optional handle, optional goal, local workspace name.\
Step 3: Data scope explanation. Explain what sources exist from ctxorg scan: files, workstreams, narratives, exposures, topology.\
Step 4: Source approval. Let user approve all workstreams or select specific workstreams. This writes consent receipts.\
Step 5: Audience selection. Load the 9 configured V1 audience lenses from the package, not hardcoded frontend strings. Allow multi-select.\
Step 6: Inference mode selection. Options can include quick, standard, deep. If only one mode works, use standard but structure for future modes.\
Step 7: Model check. Verify Ollama status and configured model availability. If unavailable, show what will be limited.\
Step 8: Run initial audit. Create an audit run and start pipeline.\
Onboarding completion must persist. If the user refreshes, they resume where they left off. If onboarding is complete, dashboard should show audit status and next action. The user should be able to revisit onboarding/settings to change scope.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 8. Consent ledger\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 Implement a real consent ledger. Consent is not text. Consent is state.\
Each consent receipt should track: receipt ID, user/profile ID, source or workstream ID, scope, allowed processing modes, selected audience contexts, retention preference, approved boolean, approval timestamp, revoked boolean, revoked timestamp, revocation reason if available, deletion requested boolean, and version.\
The inference pipeline must check consent before loading sources. Approved sources can be processed. Revoked/unapproved sources must be skipped. If a source was used in an old report, the old report can remain as a historical snapshot only if retention policy allows; otherwise deletion should remove or redact it. Build the simplest version now: revoked sources are excluded from future audits and marked in source ledger. Prepare deletion hooks for deeper cleanup.\
The UI must include a sources and consent screen showing scanned workstreams, file counts, exposure counts, last scan time, consent state, processing state, and whether each source was included in last audit. The user must be able to approve/revoke per source or workstream.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 9. Source system\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 The current filesystem scan and workstream clusters are first-class sources. Map existing ctxorg objects into V1 objects:\
FilesystemRoot becomes SourceProvider or SourceGroup.\
Workstream becomes SourceGroup/Workstream node.\
File becomes Artifact.\
File path, extension, modified time, size, inferred domain, and cluster membership become artifact metadata.\
Existing narratives become NarrativeArtifact or WorkstreamSummary, but they are not evidence by themselves unless linked to underlying files.\
Existing exposures become RiskSignal or Exposure node.\
Topology nodes become graph seed nodes.\
Iris chat history, if relevant, can become optional internal context only with consent. Do not automatically include private chat logs as evidence unless the user approves that source.\
Add source provider interfaces for future connectors: LocalFilesystemProvider, ManualURLProvider, GitHubProvider, BrowserHistoryProvider, ResumeUploadProvider, GoogleTakeoutProvider, GmailExportProvider, CalendarICSProvider, PublicSearchProvider, ScreenshotProvider, WebsiteSnapshotProvider, SocialArchiveProvider. V1.0 only needs local filesystem/workstreams working, but the interfaces should be clean.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 10. Scrapers and external collection\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 Implement scrapers responsibly and locally where possible. Do not build stealth scraping, CAPTCHA bypass, account takeover, credential stuffing, hidden browser automation, or terms-violating mass collection. Optic is a self-audit tool. Scrapers must be user-triggered, scope-limited, rate-limited, logged, and attributable to the user\'92s own authorized inquiry.\
Scraper categories:\
Manual URL scraper: user enters a URL, app fetches page HTML, extracts title, text, metadata, links, canonical URL, screenshot if local browser tooling exists, and stores it as an artifact.\
Website snapshot scraper: user enters their own domain or profile site, app crawls within domain up to a configured depth/page limit. Respect robots where applicable. Store snapshots and extracted text.\
Search result scraper/provider: use an API adapter for Brave Search, SerpAPI, Google Programmable Search, Tavily, or another search API. If no key is available, build the provider interface and disabled UI. Do not scrape Google HTML directly as the main path.\
GitHub provider: public GitHub API can work unauthenticated at low rate, but add optional GitHub token support. Pull repos, README files, pinned repos if available, commits metadata, languages, stars, forks, issues/PR summary if reasonable. For private repos, require token and explicit consent.\
LinkedIn: do not build a stealth scraper. Use user-uploaded LinkedIn export, manual profile URL snapshot if publicly accessible, or future official/OAuth route if available. If no compliant access, show import instructions.\
X/Twitter: prefer user-uploaded archive. If API key exists, implement adapter. Do not rely on brittle unauthorized scraping.\
Reddit: public API or user export where feasible.\
Browser history: local upload/import only. Do not read browser profiles silently. User must select/export/approve.\
Google Takeout: user uploads archive/folder. Parse locally.\
Gmail: V1.0 should prefer MBOX/export import. Live Gmail requires OAuth credentials and must notify user only for Google client ID/secret and configured redirect URI.\
Calendar: ICS upload first. Google Calendar OAuth later.\
Reverse image search: adapter only unless API key is provided. Do not build shady scraping. Notify for API key if this feature is requested/enabled.\
Data broker discovery: only self-audit, API/provider adapter, disabled without key, with strong warnings. Do not build people-search abuse flows.\
Required API keys to notify user for only when needed: GitHub token for higher rate/private repos; Google OAuth client ID/secret for Gmail/Drive/Calendar; Google Programmable Search API key and CX or SerpAPI key or Brave Search API key for search snapshots; Tavily API key if used for web extraction/search; X API key if official X integration is attempted; Reddit API credentials if official Reddit integration is attempted; AWS credentials only when preparing actual cloud deployment, not for local build; S3/R2 credentials only if external artifact storage is enabled; Stripe keys only for billing; email provider key only if transactional emails are built.\
Again: if the key is missing, build adapter + disabled state + clear notification. Do not stop local app completion.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 11. Audit pipeline\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 Implement the full audit pipeline as staged operations. The stages must be visible in UI and persistent in backend state.\
Stage 1: Load audit config: user/profile, selected sources, selected workstreams, selected audience lenses, inference depth, model settings.\
Stage 2: Consent filter: exclude unapproved/revoked sources.\
Stage 3: Artifact selection: load artifacts from selected workstreams/sources; apply file type filters and limits if needed.\
Stage 4: Evidence extraction: create parsed spans from file metadata, summaries, text snippets, path semantics, workstream summaries, exposure records, and existing scan outputs. If raw file content is unavailable or too expensive to parse, use available metadata but label evidence quality accordingly.\
Stage 5: Signal extraction: infer low-level signals only from evidence. Examples: project density, topic clusters, stale files, exposed secrets patterns if exposure scanner exists, public/private mismatch, workstream fragmentation, high-volume unfinished work, repeated domains, technical stack evidence, identity labels, timeline gaps.\
Stage 6: Audience application: apply selected V1 audience lens configs to signals and evidence.\
Stage 7: Inference card generation: use the existing V1 evidence-gated generator. Reject unevidenced cards. Store cards.\
Stage 8: Contradiction detection: run all 5 contradiction types from V1 package against artifacts/signals/cards/workstreams. Store contradictions.\
Stage 9: Risk/exposure mapping: map existing exposures plus new risks into RiskFlag objects.\
Stage 10: Repair generation: create concrete repair actions from cards, contradictions, and risks. Store repair queue.\
Stage 11: Report assembly: create persisted report snapshot with scope, source ledger, cards, contradictions, risks, repairs, graph summary, and exports.\
Stage 12: Graph projection: update topology/evidence graph with V1 nodes/edges or generate a graph view for the report.\
Stage 13: Iris context indexing: make audit outputs queryable by Iris chat.\
Stage 14: Completion: set audit state to completed or failed with stage-specific error.\
The UI must show these states. A failed stage must not look like success. A partial run can persist partial outputs, but it must be clearly labeled.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 12. Inference depth modes\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 Implement at least the interface for inference depth modes. If all modes share implementation initially, structure them cleanly.\
Quick mode: fewer artifacts per workstream, metadata-heavy, fewer cards, lower LLM usage, fast scan.\
Standard mode: balanced artifact selection, all selected audience lenses, contradictions, risks, repairs, report.\
Deep mode: larger artifact sample, more cross-workstream linking, stronger contradiction pass, more repair actions, more Iris context indexing, optional local LLM summarization per workstream.\
If Deep mode is expensive, make it work locally with caps and progress. Do not pretend Deep mode exists if it simply routes to the same shallow endpoint without indicating limits.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 13. Audience lenses\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 Use the 9 audience lenses from the V1 package as canonical configs. The frontend must fetch them from the backend/package. Do not duplicate config by hand in the UI.\
Each audience lens must influence inference. It is not a label. It should affect relevance weights, evidence preferences, false positive warnings, severity, and repair style. Example: recruiter lens values role clarity, timeline coherence, proof of competence, professionalism, and low ambiguity. Investor lens values execution velocity, insight, market obsession, technical leverage, coherence under chaos, and trust repair. Creator lens values public narrative, controversy surface, audience legibility, distinctiveness, and platform risk. Privacy lens values exposure, location leakage, sensitive traces, hidden metadata, account linkage, and removability.\
The UI should show selected lens chips, lens descriptions, and lens-specific output. Cards should display which lens produced or prioritized them.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 14. Contradiction engine\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 Wire all 5 contradiction detection types into the audit pipeline. A contradiction must include: contradiction ID, type, title, description, involved artifact IDs, involved span IDs if available, involved claims/signals if available, severity, confidence, affected audience lenses, suggested repair, created timestamp, and status.\
Contradiction statuses: active, ignored, resolved, needs review.\
Expose contradictions in three places: dedicated contradiction screen, report section, and linked inside inference cards/repair actions. Empty state must say checks completed and zero contradictions found. Do not silently hide contradictions because they are inconvenient.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 15. Risk and exposure engine\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 The existing ctxorg exposures must become first-class V1 risks. Map exposure outputs to RiskFlag objects. Also generate new risk flags from inference and contradictions.\
Risk categories should include: stale context, identity mismatch, timeline gap, professional incoherence, overexposure, sensitive leakage, location leakage, financial signal leakage, health/mental-state inference risk, political/religious inference risk, controversy surface, association risk, search result contamination, impersonation/confusion risk, dead profile risk, unclaimed profile risk, platform bucket risk, audience mismatch, tone mismatch, missing proof, old username risk, public metadata risk, credential ambiguity, narrative fragmentation, machine-summary distortion, secret/token exposure, local file path leakage, private project leakage, r\'e9sum\'e9/profile mismatch, abandoned repo risk, and excessive unfinished-work signal.\
Each risk must include severity, confidence, affected sources, affected audience lenses, evidence references, and repair relationship.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 16. Repair engine\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 Build a real repair engine. It must produce actions from claims, contradictions, and risks. It cannot simply say \'93improve your profile.\'94 Every repair action must be executable.\
Repair action fields: ID, audit run ID, title, summary, exact action, linked cards, linked contradictions, linked risks, source references, audience lenses affected, priority, effort, expected impact, reversibility, urgency, status, created timestamp, updated timestamp.\
Statuses: proposed, accepted, in progress, ignored, completed.\
Priority formula should approximate:\
Priority = impact \'d7 confidence \'d7 audience importance \'d7 urgency \'d7 reversibility \'f7 effort\
The repair queue UI must allow filtering by priority, effort, audience lens, source/workstream, risk type, status, and quick wins. It must support status changes and persist them.\
Repair examples for filesystem/workstream-native Optic: consolidate fragmented project folders, add README to project, remove exposed secret file, rename confusing folder, archive dead prototype, create public summary of serious workstream, separate joke/chaos files from professional portfolio, update r\'e9sum\'e9 evidence, add canonical project page, document execution history, remove stale screenshots, mark private folders as excluded from future audits, create \'93proof pack\'94 for investor lens, create \'93role clarity pack\'94 for recruiter lens, or ignore because the weirdness is strategic under founder lens.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 17. Report engine\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 Build persisted reports as snapshots. A report is not a generated text blob. It is a structured object with sections.\
Required report sections: scope and consent, selected audience lenses, source/workstream ledger, audit summary, top inference cards, confidence distribution, contradiction summary, risks/exposures, repair queue, graph/topology summary, model/settings summary, export metadata.\
Report must be viewable after reload. It must link to cards, contradictions, risks, repairs, and sources. It must have evidence drawers. It must export at least JSON and Markdown. PDF/HTML export is optional but useful if already easy.\
Markdown export must be readable and include: title, generated time, scope, selected audiences, source ledger summary, findings by audience, contradictions, risks, repairs, and caveats. JSON export must preserve structured IDs and relationships.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 18. Graph and topology\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 Preserve the existing topology graph. Extend it into V1 graph projection.\
Graph modes should be prepared:\
Workstream topology: existing clusters and file relationships.\
Evidence graph: sources \uc0\u8594  artifacts \u8594  parsed spans \u8594  signals \u8594  claims.\
Audience graph: audience lenses \uc0\u8594  prioritized claims \u8594  risks/repairs.\
Contradiction graph: conflicting claims/signals/sources.\
Repair graph: repair actions \uc0\u8594  affected risks/cards/sources.\
If full graph modes are too much for V1.0, implement at least a V1 overlay that connects workstreams to cards, contradictions, risks, and repairs. Do not leave topology disconnected from V1 outputs.\
Avoid random hairball graph. Add filters: source/workstream, audience lens, node type, confidence, severity, repair status. Clicking a graph node should open the corresponding detail panel where possible.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 19. Iris chat integration\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 Iris must become audit-aware. Keep the existing Ollama-powered Iris chat, but wire it to structured audit context.\
Iris should be able to answer: what did this audit find, which sources were used, what are the top risks, which contradictions matter, why did this inference card appear, what evidence supports it, what repairs should I do first, what changed since last audit if multiple reports exist, what did the recruiter lens see, what did the investor lens see, which workstream looks strongest, which workstream creates exposure, what is missing from the evidence.\
Iris must cite structured object IDs or names when possible. It must not invent new claims outside the structured inference pipeline unless clearly labeled as conversational speculation. Better: if Iris identifies a possible new claim, it should suggest rerunning or adding it as a candidate, not silently mutate the report.\
If Ollama is unavailable, Iris panel should show model unavailable and still allow browsing structured outputs.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 20. UI screens\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

